WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.520
The worm found two readers for one cue.

2
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.620
Do vertebrate growth cones use the same ones?

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:13.973
Back on the spinal cord map, follow the commissural axons to the floor plate.

4
00:00:14.967 --> 00:00:19.827
The worm's toward reader, UNC-40, turned out to resemble a human protein.

5
00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:24.007
Its name is DCC, Deleted in Colorectal Cancer.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:27.330
It was first studied as a cancer gene.

7
00:00:28.283 --> 00:00:35.743
In 1996, Tessier-Lavigne's laboratory tested DCC in rat embryos, spinal explants and

8
00:00:35.743 --> 00:00:38.443
cultured cells. They showed three things.

9
00:00:39.417 --> 00:00:42.037
First, DCC is on commissural axons.

10
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.890
Second, netrin-1 binds cultured cells that display DCC.

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.300
Third, they added an antibody that blocks DCC to spinal explants with netrin.

12
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:55.520
What should happen to the outgrowth?

13
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:06.573
The antibody selectively blocked netrin-evoked outgrowth from the spinal explants.

14
00:01:10.017 --> 00:01:15.117
In the same journal issue, worm work showed that UNC-40 is needed in the moving

15
00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:16.117
cells themselves.

16
00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.990
The mouse confirmed both partners.

17
00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:24.390
Mice with little or no netrin-1 had misrouted spinal commissural axons.

18
00:01:25.383 --> 00:01:27.943
They also lacked several forebrain commissures.

19
00:01:29.003 --> 00:01:31.143
Mice lacking DCC had similar defects.

20
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:37.117
Remove the cue or remove the reader, and commissural axons fail in much the same

21
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:38.117
way.

22
00:01:38.317 --> 00:01:45.637
Now repulsion. In 1995, netrin-1 repelled trochlear motor axons in explant cultures.

23
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.290
So the second puzzle is real in vertebrates.

24
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.730
Different growth cones, same cue, opposite turns.

25
00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.170
The worm already suggested why: different readers.

26
00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:02.323
In the netrin-1 mutant, though, trochlear axons were largely normal.

27
00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:04.803
Other repellents also guide them.

28
00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:08.357
The fly shows how the repulsive reader works.

29
00:02:09.197 --> 00:02:12.777
Its Unc5 receptor is a repulsive netrin receptor.

30
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:17.407
Unc5 alone is enough for short-range repulsion.

31
00:02:18.427 --> 00:02:23.107
Long-range repulsion also needs Frazzled, the fly's DCC-family receptor.

32
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.530
So the receptor layer reads like this.

33
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:29.070
DCC draws a growth cone in.

34
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.890
UNC5 receptors add repulsion.

35
00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.750
Treat that as a starting point.

36
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:39.250
DCC-family receptors also take part in some repulsion.

37
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.490
And netrin has other receptors and partners.

38
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:48.423
The takeaway: the cue is shared, and the receptors a growth cone carries set the

39
00:02:48.423 --> 00:02:49.023
first answer.
